WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.043
Here's a card payment, named auth:2.

2
00:00:05.043 --> 00:00:11.225
When the bank said yes, the database saved it as PENDING: approved, but no money has moved yet.

3
00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:15.478
A minute later, the money moved, and the database saved CAPTURED.

4
00:00:15.478 --> 00:00:21.044
Both records are on disk now, in two different files, and neither file was ever changed.

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.056
So a program asks for auth:2.

6
00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:24.591
Which answer comes back?

7
00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:27.791
And how does the database know it's the right one?

8
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:32.491
To see how two answers got there, picture a clerk with a pile of forms.

9
00:00:32.491 --> 00:00:36.787
Filing each form in its drawer means a walk across the room, per form.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.948
That would make her the fittest clerk in the building, and the slowest.

11
00:00:40.948 --> 00:00:44.117
So she keeps a tray on her desk, in order by form number.

12
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:50.104
When the tray is full, she binds it into a booklet and puts it on the shelf, newest in front.

13
00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:52.164
A bound booklet is never changed.

14
00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:55.468
A correction goes into the tray, like any new form.

15
00:00:55.468 --> 00:00:59.771
And to cancel a form, she adds a slip to the tray that says cancelled.

16
00:00:59.967 --> 00:01:00.893
Let's map it.

17
00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:06.380
The tray is the memtable: a sorted list in memory that takes every new write.

18
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:11.480
A booklet is an SSTable: a sorted file on disk that never changes.

19
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.507
And the cancel slip is a tombstone, a record that says this key is deleted.

20
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:19.714
Now the real thing, with three payments.

21
00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:22.989
auth:1, auth:2 and auth:3 arrive, all PENDING.

22
00:01:22.989 --> 00:01:25.979
Each goes into the memtable, in key order.

23
00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:27.688
No file on disk changes.

24
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:33.895
The memtable reaches its size limit, so it's written to disk in key order, in one long write.

25
00:01:33.895 --> 00:01:37.331
That's called a flush, and the new file is SSTable 1.

26
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:41.729
Then auth:2's money moves, so CAPTURED goes into the memtable.

27
00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:44.891
And auth:3 is deleted, so a tombstone goes in.

28
00:01:44.891 --> 00:01:46.541
SSTable 1 isn't touched.

29
00:01:46.541 --> 00:01:48.672
It still says PENDING for both.

30
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.809
Another flush makes SSTable 2.

31
00:01:50.809 --> 00:01:54.826
So the disk holds two versions of auth:2, and two of auth:3.

32
00:01:54.826 --> 00:01:58.441
Only the order of the files says which one is current.

33
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:01.180
So how should a read search the files?

34
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:07.847
One idea feels natural: start at the oldest, like reading a book from page one, and take the first match.

35
00:02:08.067 --> 00:02:10.062
Let's try it for auth:2.

36
00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:12.224
SSTable 1 has it: PENDING.

37
00:02:12.224 --> 00:02:12.639
Done?

38
00:02:12.639 --> 00:02:13.221
Well...

39
00:02:13.221 --> 00:02:15.549
that's last minute's answer.

40
00:02:15.549 --> 00:02:17.877
The money has already moved.

41
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:19.557
What would the clerk do?

42
00:02:19.557 --> 00:02:26.119
She checks the tray first, then the booklets from the front of the shelf, and stops at the first one that has the form.

43
00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:29.922
So a read of auth:2 checks the memtable first.

44
00:02:29.922 --> 00:02:30.717
Not there.

45
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.260
Then SSTable 2, the newest file.

46
00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.167
It has auth:2: CAPTURED.

47
00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:36.359
The read stops.

48
00:02:36.359 --> 00:02:40.173
SSTable 1, with its old PENDING, is never asked.

49
00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:43.192
A read of auth:1 checks the memtable.

50
00:02:43.192 --> 00:02:43.965
Not there.

51
00:02:43.965 --> 00:02:50.687
SSTable 2 doesn't have it either, and finding that out costs a block read, for nothing.

52
00:02:50.687 --> 00:02:52.541
SSTable 1 does: PENDING.

53
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.998
Now auth:3, the payment that was deleted.

54
00:02:55.998 --> 00:02:57.660
The memtable is empty.

55
00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.345
SSTable 2 holds a tombstone, and SSTable 1 still says PENDING.

56
00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:04.460
What should the read answer?

57
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.669
Pause and guess.

58
00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:09.563
Not found.

59
00:03:09.563 --> 00:03:11.789
The read stops at the tombstone.

60
00:03:11.789 --> 00:03:18.679
If it carried on, SSTable 1 would hand back PENDING, and a deleted payment would come back to life.

61
00:03:18.867 --> 00:03:20.813
Tombstone is a good name for it.

62
00:03:20.813 --> 00:03:24.340
It marks the spot, and it tells you not to dig any deeper.

63
00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.084
So the first file that knows the key wins, even when what it knows is deleted.

64
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.718
That's which file.

65
00:03:30.718 --> 00:03:33.354
Now, what does asking one file cost?

66
00:03:33.354 --> 00:03:37.454
An SSTable is records in key order, grouped into blocks.

67
00:03:37.454 --> 00:03:39.944
In the lab, a block is 16 records.

68
00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:44.204
Because the file is sorted, it doesn't need an entry for every key.

69
00:03:44.204 --> 00:03:49.186
A small index holds only the first key of each block, and where that block starts.

70
00:03:49.186 --> 00:03:50.948
That's called a sparse index.

71
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.652
The lab's file of 1,000 records needs 63 entries.

72
00:03:54.652 --> 00:03:57.407
That's small enough to keep in memory.

73
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.897
To find auth:00500, look for the last block whose first key isn't above it.

74
00:04:02.897 --> 00:04:05.934
That's the block that starts at auth:00496.

75
00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.265
Read that one block, and scan it.

76
00:04:08.265 --> 00:04:09.253
One disk read.

77
00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:12.338
So one file costs at most one read.

78
00:04:12.338 --> 00:04:13.843
But how many files?

79
00:04:13.843 --> 00:04:16.456
Picture 20 SSTables on the shelf.

80
00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:21.739
A key that only the oldest one holds costs 19 useless block reads first.

81
00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:24.504
And a key that isn't anywhere costs all 20.

82
00:04:24.504 --> 00:04:28.091
That's a lot of booklets opened to learn a form isn't there.

83
00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.531
The number of disk reads for one lookup is called read amplification.

84
00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:34.794
LSM trees fight it in two ways.

85
00:04:34.794 --> 00:04:38.849
A filter for each file lets most useless reads skip the disk.

86
00:04:38.849 --> 00:04:42.438
And compaction merges files, so the stack stays short.

87
00:04:42.438 --> 00:04:45.496
The next part of the lesson builds the filter.

88
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:50.673
So the moral of the story: files are never changed, so their order is the truth.

89
00:04:50.673 --> 00:04:54.389
Read newest first, and the first one that knows the key wins.

90
00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:55.720
One quick check.

91
00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.478
Say auth:1's money moves now, and CAPTURED goes into the memtable.

92
00:05:00.478 --> 00:05:03.723
How many SSTables does a read of auth:1 open?

93
00:05:03.723 --> 00:05:04.876
Pause and think.

94
00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:08.442
None.

95
00:05:08.442 --> 00:05:14.075
The memtable knows auth:1, so it answers from memory, and no file is asked.
